  "content": {
    "id": "p44-q24-28-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Find the domain and range of the following.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p44-q24",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\log(x-1)+\\log(x+1)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p44-q24-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "D=(1,\\infty),\\quad R=(-\\infty,\\infty)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p44-q24-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Both original arguments must be positive before combining the logarithms.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Both logarithmic arguments must be positive:\n$$\\begin{align*}x-1&>0\\\\x+1&>0.\\end{align*}$$\nTogether these require $x>1$, so $D=(1,\\infty)$.\n\nOn this domain,\n$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=\\log(x-1)+\\log(x+1)\\\\f(x)&=\\log\\bigl((x-1)(x+1)\\bigr)\\\\f(x)&=\\log(x^2-1).\\end{align*}$$\nAs $x$ varies over $(1,\\infty)$, $x^2-1$ takes every positive value. Therefore $R=(-\\infty,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q25",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\log(\\sqrt{x})$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=(0,\\infty)$; $R=(-\\infty,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "The square root must exist and the logarithmic argument must be positive:\n$$\\begin{align*}x&\\ge0\\\\\\sqrt{x}&>0\\\\x&>0.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $D=(0,\\infty)$.\n\nFor $x>0$, $\\sqrt{x}$ takes every positive value. Its logarithm therefore takes every real value, so $R=(-\\infty,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q26",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\log(x^2-1)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=(-\\infty,-1)\\cup(1,\\infty)$; $R=(-\\infty,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "Require the logarithmic argument to be positive:\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-1&>0\\\\x^2&>1.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $x<-1$ or $x>1$, giving $D=(-\\infty,-1)\\cup(1,\\infty)$.\n\nOn this domain, $x^2-1$ takes every positive value. Therefore $R=(-\\infty,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q27",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\log(1+x^2)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D=(-\\infty,\\infty)$; $R=[0,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "For every real $x$,\n$$1+x^2\\ge1>0.$$\nHence $D=(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nThe argument takes every value in $[1,\\infty)$. Since $\\log$ is increasing and $\\log(1)=0$, the range is $R=[0,\\infty)$. The minimum is attained at $x=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q28",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\log_3(9-x^2)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p44-q28-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "D=(-3,3),\\quad R=(-\\infty,2]",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p44-q28-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The argument lies in ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "(0,9]",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", with its maximum attained at ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Require the logarithmic argument to be positive:\n$$\\begin{align*}9-x^2&>0\\\\x^2&<9.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $-3<x<3$, giving $D=(-3,3)$.\n\nOn this domain, $9-x^2$ takes every value in $(0,9]$. The base-3 logarithm is increasing, and\n$$\\log_3(9)=2.$$\nThe maximum is attained at $x=0$, while the logarithm decreases without bound as the argument approaches zero. Hence $R=(-\\infty,2]$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 108
      }
    ]
  },
